      <h2 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal leading-[1.15] mb-4" style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);">
        How much does each type of doula cost?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-8">
        Doula prices depend on what kind of support you need. Birth doulas, postpartum doulas, and night doulas each have different pricing structures. Understanding these differences helps you budget accurately for the support you want.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal leading-[1.15] mb-4" style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);">
        How Much Does a Birth Doula Cost?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-8">
        If you are wondering <strong>how much does a birth doula cost</strong>, the short answer is $650 to $3,000 for a full birth package, with most families paying between $1,200 and $1,800. The price hinges on where you live, the doula's experience level, and what the package includes — prenatal visits, on-call availability from 37 weeks, continuous labor support, and one or two postpartum check-ins. Below is a closer look at birth doula pricing, followed by postpartum and night doula rates for comparison.
      </p>

      <img src="/images/doula-meeting.webp" alt="A doula meeting with expectant parents to discuss birth preferences and support options" class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div class="border-b border-tjb-lavender-300/40 pb-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-xl mb-2">Birth doula cost: $650 to $3,000</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Birth doulas charge a flat package fee rather than an hourly rate. The package covers prenatal visits, on-call availability from 37 weeks, continuous labor support, and 1-2 postpartum visits. In major metros, birth doula costs reach $1,500 to $3,000. In Texas cities like Dallas, Houston, and Austin, expect $800 to $2,800. Smaller towns and rural areas run $650 to $1,500. The national average doula cost for a birth package is around $1,200 to $1,500.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-b border-tjb-lavender-300/40 pb-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-xl mb-2">Postpartum doula cost: $25 to $50 per hour</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Postpartum doulas charge hourly, typically with a four-hour minimum per visit. The hourly rate ranges from $25 to $50 depending on location and experience. A typical postpartum doula package runs $400 to $1,200 for a series of visits over the first few weeks. Postpartum doulas help with recovery, infant feeding, light household tasks, and emotional support. For a detailed breakdown, see our <a href="/postpartum-doula-cost/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:underline">postpartum doula cost guide</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-b border-tjb-lavender-300/40 pb-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-xl mb-2">Night doula cost: $35 to $75 per hour</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Night doulas specialize in overnight newborn care, letting parents sleep. They typically charge $35 to $75 per hour, more than daytime postpartum doulas, because overnight work commands a premium. A typical night shift runs 8 to 10 hours, so a single night costs $280 to $750. Most families use night doulas 3 to 6 nights per week for the first 2 to 6 weeks, bringing weekly costs to $840 to $4,500 depending on frequency and rate. Night doula costs add up quickly, but for families without local support or recovering from a difficult birth, the sleep they buy can be worth every dollar. For comparison, see our <a href="/postpartum-doula-cost/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:underline">postpartum doula cost guide</a> for daytime rates.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-xl mb-2">Hourly vs. package: which makes sense?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Birth doulas almost always charge a flat package fee. Postpartum and night doulas charge hourly. If you only need a few postpartum visits, hourly makes sense. If you want continuous support from pregnancy through early postpartum, look for a combined package, which may offer better value than booking separately.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

