          Many parents find that price correlates strongly with experience, but the personal connection matters just as much. In practice, we've heard from families who interviewed both a newer doula charging $1,200 and a veteran charging $3,200 in the same city. The experienced doula came with a confidence and depth of knowledge that made the higher price feel worth it. One mother described her experienced doula as someone who could practically guarantee she would feel amazing about her birth experience, while the less expensive option seemed less certain during the interview.
        </p>
        <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Sliding scale doulas exist, and they are worth asking about even if you don't see one advertised. It can happen that a doula volunteers a payment plan before a family even raises cost as a concern. In markets like New York, some doulas openly offer sliding scale pricing from $2,000 to $2,500, with flexible payment arrangements for families who need them.
