        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-4 text-center border border-tjb-lavender-200/60">
          <div class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-tjb-rose-500 mb-1">15%</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-tjb-gray leading-tight">more likely to have a vaginal birth</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-4 text-center border border-tjb-lavender-200/60">
          <div class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-tjb-rose-500 mb-1">35%</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-tjb-gray leading-tight">less likely to report a negative birth experience</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The research is clear: continuous doula support leads to better outcomes. A <a href="https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD003766.pub5/full" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:underline" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Cochrane review of 26 trials</a> found that people with continuous doula support were <strong>39% less likely to have a C-section</strong>, <strong>15% more likely to have a spontaneous vaginal birth</strong>, and <strong>35% less likely to report a negative birth experience</strong>. Doula care has also been linked to shorter labors and lower rates of epidural use.
      </p>
      <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-6">
        When you consider that a C-section can add $5,000 to $10,000+ to hospital costs, the doula fee that helps you avoid one is a fraction of that. Beyond the financial math, families consistently report higher satisfaction and confidence when they have continuous support during labor.
