  <section class="bg-white py-16 md:py-20">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">

      <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Doula cost by state &amp; situation</p>
      <div class="w-10 h-0.5 bg-tjb-rose-500 mb-6"></div>
      <h2 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal leading-[1.15] mb-4" style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);">
        Doula Cost by State &amp; Situation
      </h2>
      <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-8">
        Cost is the most common question families ask before hiring a doula, and the honest answer is that it depends on three things: which state or city you live in, whether you're paying with or without insurance, and which type of doula support you want. Below we answer the exact questions families search for most, with the same real 2025-2026 market figures used throughout this guide.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="bg-tjb-cream rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much do doulas charge per client?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Most birth doulas charge a flat package fee per client rather than an hourly rate. That package typically costs <strong>$650 to $3,000</strong> per family, with most doulas charging <strong>$1,200 to $1,500</strong> for a standard birth package. The per-client price covers 1-2 prenatal visits, on-call availability from around 37 weeks, continuous labor support during the birth, and 1-2 postpartum visits. Postpartum doulas are the exception — they charge by the hour, usually $25 to $50 per hour, rather than a per-client package. When you hire a birth doula, you're not paying for a single meeting; you're paying for the full arc of prenatal prep, labor, and early postpartum support.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-lavender-50/40 rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">What is the average doula cost in the US in 2025?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            The <strong>average doula cost in the US</strong> for a birth package is around <strong>$1,200 to $1,500</strong> as of 2025. That national average hides real regional differences: in major metros like New York City, Los Angeles, and San Francisco the average runs $1,800 to $2,500, while in mid-size markets like Dallas, Houston, and Atlanta it's closer to $1,200 to $1,800, and in smaller towns and rural areas it falls to $800 to $1,200. Prices have been rising across most markets — roughly 15 to 25 percent since 2020 — as demand for doula support continues to grow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-cream rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much is a doula in California?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            In California, a birth doula typically costs <strong>$2,000 to $3,000</strong>, and postpartum doulas run higher than the national average at $55 to $75 per hour. California's cost of living and large metro areas push prices to the top of the national range, with Los Angeles and San Francisco doulas commonly charging $2,000 to $3,000 for a full birth package. One upside: <strong>Medi-Cal now reimburses doula services statewide</strong>, so eligible families can get doula care covered. For local detail, see our <a href="/birth-support/ca/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:underline font-medium">California birth support guides →</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-lavender-50/40 rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much is a doula in Georgia?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            In Georgia, doula pricing is more moderate than the West Coast. A birth doula in Atlanta typically costs <strong>$800 to $2,200</strong>, and postpartum doulas in the Atlanta area charge $25 to $45 per hour. Georgia's Southeast market tends to sit below the national average for many families, especially compared with states like California or New York. The exact figure varies by city and by the doula's experience level, so it's worth getting quotes from a few local doulas to compare. For local guidance, browse our <a href="/birth-support/ga/" class="text-tjb-rose-500 hover:underline font-medium">Georgia birth support guides →</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-cream rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much does a doula cost with insurance?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            With insurance, the cost of a doula depends entirely on your plan. Some private insurers now cover doula services as part of maternal wellness benefits, and Medicaid coverage is expanding state by state — California's Medi-Cal reimburses doulas statewide, and Texas Medicaid covers doula services under SB 750. When insurance does cover doula care, your out-of-pocket cost can drop to little or nothing. But coverage varies widely, even within the same insurance company, and results often come down to which representative you speak to. If a claim is denied the first time, it's worth calling back and asking about in-network exceptions or your plan's maternal health benefits specifically. Many families also use FSA or HSA funds, which can make doula care effectively tax-free. Check directly with your plan to confirm what's covered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-lavender-50/40 rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much do doulas cost without insurance?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Without insurance, most families pay the full doula fee out of pocket: <strong>$650 to $3,000</strong> for a birth package, averaging $1,200 to $1,500. This is the reality for the majority of families, since doula care is often not covered by health insurance. There are ways to make it more affordable even without coverage. Many doulas offer sliding-scale fees based on income, payment plans, or reduced rates for student doulas completing certification. Community-based doula collectives and volunteer programs can offer lower-cost or free support. It's always worth asking a doula you love whether they offer a sliding scale, even if they don't advertise one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-lavender-50/40 rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much do doulas usually cost? How much do doulas cost on average?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Doulas usually cost <strong>$1,200 to $1,500</strong> for a birth package, and that's also the national average. The full typical range is $650 to $3,000 depending on where you live and how experienced your doula is. So "how much are doulas" has a short answer — usually around $1,200 to $1,500 — and a longer one that depends on your market. In major metros doulas typically run $1,500 to $3,000, in Texas cities $800 to $2,800, and in smaller towns and rural areas $650 to $1,500. Postpartum doulas cost $25 to $50 per hour on average.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-tjb-cream rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/50">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">How much is a birth doula?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            A birth doula costs <strong>$650 to $3,000</strong> for a full birth package, with most families paying <strong>$1,200 to $1,500</strong>. The price covers 1-2 prenatal visits, on-call availability from around 37 weeks, continuous support through labor, and 1-2 postpartum visits. Birth doulas charge a flat package fee rather than an hourly rate, which is different from postpartum doulas who charge $25 to $50 per hour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-tjb-lavender-200/60">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal text-lg mb-2">Is it "doula" or "dula"?</h3>
          <p class="text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed mb-2">
            It's spelled <strong>"doula"</strong> — the word is sometimes written "dula," but it refers to the same thing: a trained professional who provides continuous emotional, physical, and informational support during pregnancy, labor, and the early postpartum period. So when people search "how much is a dula," they're asking the same question this page answers: a birth doula costs $650 to $3,000, averaging $1,200 to $1,500. And a postpartum doula costs $25 to $50 per hour.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

